7: Survey\nL108: \nL109: cite1†yes cite1†no Was this document useful for you?\nL110: \nL111:    Thank you for your participation!\nL112: \nL113: * Your assessment is very important for improving the work of artificial intelligence, which forms the content of this project\nL114: \nL115: 1\nL116: \nL117: 2\nL118: \nL119: 3\nL120: \nL121: 4\nL122: \nL123: 5\nL124: \nL125: 6\nL126: \nL127: 7\nL128: \nL129: 8\nL130: \nL131: 9\nL132: \nL133: 10\nL134: \nL135: 11\nL136: \nL137: cite82†iframe L138: \nL139: Document related concepts\nL140: \nL141: no text concepts found\nL142: Transcript\nL143: \nL144:     MATHEMATICS OF COMPUTATION\nL145:     VOLUME 48. NUMBER 177\nL146:     JANUARY 1987, PAGES 329-339\nL147:     The Multiple Polynomial Quadratic Sieve\nL148:     By Robert D. Silverman\nL149:     For Daniel Shanks on the occasion of his 10 th birthday\nL150:     Abstract. A modification, due to Peter Montgomery, of Pomerance's Quadratic Sieve for\nL151:     factoring large integers is discussed along with its implementation. Using it, allows factorization with over an order of magnitude less sieving than the basic algorithm. It enables one to\nL152:     factor numbers in the 60-digit range in about a day, using a large minicomputer. The\nL153:     algorithm has features which make it well adapted to parallel implementation.\nL154:     1. Introduction. The basic quadratic sieve algorithm has origins which date back to\nL155:     M. Kraitchik, but was first explicitly stated and analyzed by C. Pomerance. The only\nL156:     two reported implementations of this algorithm were done by J. Gerver at Rutgers\nL157:     and J. Davis and D. Holdridge at Sandia National Laboratories. The latter used a\nL158:     Cray-1 to factor some numbers in the 60-70 digit range from the 'Cunningham\nL159:     Project'. They also used a Cray XMP to factor the then 'MOST WANTED' number\nL160:     (1071 - l)/9 in 9.5 hours. While some of the arguments that follow have been\nL161:     detailed by Pomerance elsewhere [9], we present ours in a manner which is more\nL162:     oriented towards implementation of the algorithm. The basic algorithm depends on\nL163:     constructing a solution to the following equation, where TVis the number you wish\nL164:     to factor:\nL165:     (1)\nL166:     A2 = B2modN.\nL167:     If A # B and A # -B mod N, then (A + B, N) and (A - B, N) are proper factors\nL168:     of TV.\nL169:     This version of the quadratic sieve generates a set of quadratic residues of N using\nL170:     the following single polynomial:\nL171:     (2)\nL172:     Q(x) = (xA[fW])2-N\nL173:     = H2modN.\nL174:     It follows immediately that if a prime p \\ Q(x), then p \\ Q(x + kp) for all k e Z.\nL175:     Thus, the values of the polynomial may be factored with a sieve, once one solves\nL176:     Q(x) = 0 mod p. This may be solved by any one of a number of available\nL177:     algorithms [5, p. 437]. The potential divisors p of Q(x) are exactly those primes for\nL178:     which the Legendre symbol (N/p) = 1 and the unit -1 is needed to hold the sign.\nL179:     The algorithm now proceeds as follows:\nL180:     (i) Select a factor base FB = {pi\\(N/p¡) = l,p¡ prime, i = 1,...,F}\nL181:     for some\nL182:     appropriate value of F, and p0 = 1 for the sign.\nL183:     Received October 24, 1985; revised February 11, 1986.\nL184:     1980 MathematicsSubjectClassification.Primary10A25,10A10;Secondary10-04, 68C25.\nL185:     ©1987 American Mathematical Society\nL186:     0025-5718/87 $1.00 + $.25 per page\nL187:     329\nL188:     330\nL189:     ROBERT D. SILVERMAN\nL190:     (ii) Solve the quadratic equation Q(x) = 0 mod/7, for all pi e FB. There will be\nL191:     two roots rx and r2 for each p..\nL192:     (iii) Initialize a sieve array to zero over the interval [-M, M] for some appropriate\nL193:     M.\nL194:     (iv) For all pi e FB add the value of [log(/>,)] to the sieve array at locations rx,\nL195:     rx A Pi, rx ± 2Pi... and r2, r2 ± Pi, r2 ±2pi....\nL196:     (v) The value of Q(x) will be approximately Mf~N over [-M, M], so compare\nL197:     each sieve location with [\\og(N)/2 + log(M)]. Fully factored residues will have\nL198:     their corresponding sieve value close to this value. For these, construct the exact\nL199:     factorization via division. Factorizations are found so infrequently that the time to\nL200:     do this division is negligible. One need not check all the primes in the factor base in\nL201:     doing this division. If x is the location in the sieve array, one need only compute\nL202:     R = x mod p¡. Only if R equals one of the two roots do we go ahead and do the\nL203:     multi-precise division;\nL204:     (3)\nL205:     Q(x)-Y\\PÎ',\nL206:     i=0\nL207:     Pi^FB.\nL208:     Let Vj be the corresponding vector of exponents [aJX aj2 aj3 ■■■ ajF] with H2 =\nL209:     Q(x).\nL210:     (vi) Collect F A 1 factorizations. One then finds a set of residues whose product is\nL211:     a square via Gaussian elimination over GF(2) on the matrix formed by reducing all\nL212:     of the Vj mod 2. This creates a linear dependency mod 2 on the exponents, and the\nL213:     product of the vectors in that dependency forms a square. It is then trivial to\nL214:     construct an instance of congruence (1).\nL215:     The chief difficulty with this approach is that one must obtain approximately as\nL216:     many fully factored residues as there are primes in the factor base. In order to obtain\nL217:     enough factorizations, M must be very large, and the residues grow linearly in size\nL218:     with M.\nL219:     A way around this problem was formally suggested by Peter Montgomery: Simply\nL220:     use many polynomials to generate the residues and sieve each polynomial over a\nL221:     much smaller interval. Utilizing multiple polynomials enables one to keep the sieve\nL222:     interval small, and hence makes the residues easier to factor. We have implemented\nL223:     this approach and found that it is quite effective. It allows one to find enough\nL224:     factored residues using less than one-tenth the total sieve length in the single\nL225:     polynomial version. We also show that the cost of changing polynomials is small.\nL226:     In fact, the implementation of Sandia was done in two stages. Their first version\nL227:     used a single polynomial. A later version used multiple polynomials, but in a\nL228:     disguised rather than explicit form. This latter version created implicit polynomials\nL229:     from subsequences of the main sieve which were divisible by a large prime q lying\nL230:     outside the factor base. They called their method \"special q\\\". Montgomery's\nL231:     suggested polynomials are somewhat better, however, because they yield residues\nL232:     which are smaller on average and are less clumsy to implement.\nL233:     In Section 2 we shall discuss how to select the coefficients of the polynomials.\nL234:     Section 3 will show how to compute those coefficients efficiently. Section 4 will\nL235:     discuss the basic steps of the algorithm. Section 5 will discuss selection of the\nL236:     THE MULTIPLE POLYNOMIAL QUADRATIC SIEVE\nL237:     331\nL238:     algorithm's input parameters. Section 6 will present some numerical results. In\nL239:     Section 7 we discuss its parallel implementation and finally, in Section 8, we\nL240:     compare our algorithm with other methods.\nL241:     2. Selection of Coefficients. Select Q(x) = Ax2 A Bx A C. In order to make\nL242:     Q(x) generate quadratic residues, it is required that B2 - 4AC = N. Since this\nL243:     latter expression is congruent to 0 or 1 mod 4, it means that if N = 3 mod 4 one\nL244:     must premultiply it by a small constant k, so kN = 1 mod 4. This is also a good\nL245:     thing to do, in general, because it usually allows one to find a factor base which is\nL246:     much richer in small primes. We will later discuss a function that may be used to\nL247:     evaluate a multiplier. A suggestion by Pomerance [8] allows one to do away with\nL248:     requiring kN = 1 mod 4: Simply take 25 as the middle coefficient rather than B.\nL249:     However, we have not yet implemented this suggestion. It would be advantageous to\nL250:     keep the value of Q(x) small, in some appropriate sense, over [-M, M]. There are\nL251:     several obvious ways of doing this, e.g.,\nL252:     (a)\nL253:     Minimize sup | Q ( x ) | over [ -M, M ]\nL254:     /M-M\nL255:     \\Q(x)\\dx\nL256:     (c) Minimize I Q2(x)dx\nL257:     subject to\nL258:     (d) B2-4AC = kN and A,B,C(=Z.\nL259:     It is easily seen that (4a) and (4b) are essentially equivalent. The length of the base\nL260:     of the parabola is 2 M and its area will be directly proportional to its height.\nL261:     Relaxing the integer constraints and solving each of the above Lagrange multiplier\nL262:     problems, one finds that they all yield essentially the same result. The exact answer\nL263:     for (4a) is\nL264:     A = WxfkÑ/M,\nL265:     (5)\nL266:     5 = 0,\nL267:     C= W2M4k~Ñ,\nL268:     where\nL269:     Wx = v/2/2\nL270:     and\nL271:     W2= -l/2\\/2\nL272:     .\nL273:     The only difference among the results of the different minimization problems is\nL274:     that the constants Wx and W2change very slightly. Wx ranges from .70 to .75 and W2\nL275:     ranges from -.3 to -.35 with WXW2= - \\.\nL276:     The maximum value of Q(x) over [-M, M] is MfkW/2f2,\nL277:     a factor of >/8\nL278:     improvement over (2) and the 'special q' polynomials at Sandia.\nL279:     B = 0 follows immediately from symmetry considerations, but (4a) and (4c) give\nL280:     similar results for A sind C because the constraint B2 - 4AC = kN is very binding\nL281:     on the shape of Q(x). The simplest way to see this is to realize that at the roots its\nL282:     slope will be ± fkÑ. We would really like to flatten the parabola, but the constraint\nL283:     on the discriminant means that the curve must have a certain 'steepness'. Thus, we\nL284:     can do little more than translate the parabola up and down.\nL285:     332\nL286:     ROBERT D. SILVERMAN\nL287:     3. Computation of Coefficients. A simple method for selecting A, B, and C comes\nL288:     from a method for finding modular square roots quickly. To satisfy (4d) one must\nL289:     have\nL290:     (6)\nL291:     B2 = kN mod4A.\nL292:     Let A = D2, (D/kN) = 1, D = 3 mod4 and A ~ y/kN/2/M. It is desirable\nL293:     that D be prime because if a prime in the factor base divides A, then Q(x) = 0\nL294:     mod p has only one root and the probability that p \\ Q(x) over [-M, M] drops from\nL295:     2/p to 1/p. It is sufficient for practical purposes that D be only a probable prime.\nL296:     Alternatively, one may select D to be the product of primes not in the factor base,\nL297:     but its factorization must be known to solve (6). Our implementation took D to be a\nL298:     probable prime. To find the coefficients, compute\nL299:     (7a)\nL300:     h0=(kN)iD-3)/4modD,\nL301:     (7b)\nL302:     hx = kNh0 = (kN)(D+1)/4modD.\nL303:     Then\nL304:     (8)\nL305:     Let\nL306:     h\\ = kN(kN){D~1)/2modi) = kN modD\nL307:     (9)\nL308:     h2 = (2hxyl\nL309:     since (D/kN) = 1.\nL310:     kN - h\\\nL311:     modD.\nL312:     D\nL313:     One now has\nL314:     (10)\nL315:     said\nL316:     B = hx + h2D mod A\nL317:     (11)\nL318:     B2 = h\\ A 2hxh2D Ah\\D2 = kN mod A.\nL319:     Since B must be odd, if it is even subtract it from A.\nL320:     The value of (2/i,)_1 mod D is easily obtained since h0 = /if1 mod D has already\nL321:     been computed. One also has\nL322:     (12)\nL323:     (U)\nL324:     C \\B2-kN\nL325:     C - [ 4A\nL326:     It is not necessary, in practice, to actually compute (12) since the value of C is not\nL327:     really needed. It may be used, however, as a check on the other computations.\nL328:     Compute and save the value of 1/2D mod kN for later. This will enable us to\nL329:     quickly compute Q(x) when we find a factorization.\nL330:     As a result of the way D was chosen, one also has\nL331:     (13)\nL332:     2(*) = //2=(^f±l)2mod*/V.\nL333:     Some care must be taken: If x lies between the real roots of Q(x), then Q(x) is\nL334:     negative, and one must subtract its value from kN.\nL335:     The cost of finding the coefficients is dominated by the probable prime and\nL336:     residue tests on D, the computation of hx and l/2hx modD and 1/2D mod kN.\nL337:     Nevertheless, the total arithmetic to be done is small.\nL338:     Finally, the roots of Q(x) mod p„ p¿ e FB, are\nL339:     (14)\nL340:     (-B A v/WV)(2^)\"1mod/»,,\nL341:     since B2 — 4AC is invariant.\nL342:     THE MULTIPLE POLYNOMIAL QUADRATIC SIEVE\nL343:     333\nL344:     Most of the cost of changing polynomials occurs in computing (14). The cost of\nL345:     computing (14) is dominated by the computation of (1/2A) mod p¡, which must be\nL346:     done for all primes in the factor base. Even with an efficient algorithm for doing\nL347:     this, such as the extended Euclidean algorithm, one must typically do this thousands\nL348:     of times when changing polynomials. On a SUN-3/75, for a 60-digit number with a\nL349:     factor base of 3000 primes, it takes .9 seconds to compute the coefficients and 2.6\nL350:     seconds to compute all the roots.\nL351:     4. Description of the Algorithm. We outline here the basic steps of the algorithm,\nL352:     (i) Select a multiplier k such that kN = 1 mod 4 and kN is rich in small\nL353:     quadratic residues. We prefer .-t/V= 1 mod 8, since 2 e FB only under this condition.\nL354:     (ii) Select the size of the factor base F, the length of the sieve interval 2 M A 1,\nL355:     and a large prime tolerance T. Suggestions for the values of these parameters are\nL356:     given below.\nL357:     (iii) Compute a test value\nL358:     \\JmMK\\,\nL359:     \\ pmsix\nL360:     j\nL361:     where />max is the largest prime in the factor base. If T < 2, then whenever a value\nL362:     in the sieve exceeds this value, the corresponding value of Q(x) will be fully\nL363:     factored. If T > 2, then Q(x) may not be fully factored. However, these partial\nL364:     factorizations can also be useful, as we shall see later.\nL365:     (iv) Compute the factor base FB said fkÑ mod /?, for all /?, e FB. Compute\nL366:     [log(/>,)]forall/>, GFB.\nL367:     While (not enough factorizations)\nL368:     begin\nL369:     Generate coefficients for the polynomial.\nL370:     Solve Q(x) = 0 mod/?, for all /?, e FB.\nL371:     Do the sieving.\nL372:     Scan the sieve array: if any value exceeds the test value\nL373:     begin\nL374:     Compute Q(x) from (13) and try to find its factorization via division.\nL375:     Save the value of H and the exponent vector from the factorization of\nL376:     Ô(*).\nL377:     end\nL378:     end\nL379:     (v) Large Prime Procedure. Many of the factorizations found in (iv) will not be\nL380:     complete over the factor base. However, it has been noted that if Q(-x) is factored as\nL381:     (15)\nL382:     Q(x) = UpÏ'L,\nL383:     whereL>1,\nL384:     then, whenever we find two or more instances of the same value of L occurring, we\nL385:     may multiply the corresponding instances of (15) together. This yields a factor of L2\nL386:     on the right-hand side and we may save this result for the matrix reduction step.\nL387:     334\nL388:     ROBERT D. SILVERMAN\nL389:     Note that it is not necessary that L be prime: We only need two or more to match.\nL390:     We search for matches simply by sorting all instances of (15) using the value of L as\nL391:     the key. We refer to (15) as a large prime factorization. The value of T in (ii) allows\nL392:     one to control the size of L. We choose to keep all L's whose value lies below\nL393:     /?maxr, where /?max is the largest prime in the factor base. This can have the effect\nL394:     of more than halving the run time.\nL395:     Let FF be the number of residues fully factored over the factor base and let FT\nL396:     be the total number of factorizations with F as the size of the factor base. Then\nL397:     R=\nL398:     (16)\nL399:     FT\nL400:     F A FT- FF\nL401:     Use this to determine when enough factorizations have occurred [11]. A dependency\nL402:     can almost always be found by taking R = .96. In practice it is not necessary to\nL403:     collect F A 1 factorizations. Usually, having about .9F rows in the matrix is\nL404:     sufficient to find a dependency, and taking R = .96 achieves this.\nL405:     (vi) Matrix Reduction. Finally, collect all of the factorizations found and reduce\nL406:     the matrix over GF(2). For each linear dependency S,\nL407:     Px = Y\\Hj mod kN for ; e S,\nL408:     (17)\nL409:     '\nL410:     P2 m Y}pfjvJ'/2 mod kN for all p, e FB and j e S.\nL411:     i\nL412:     If Px # P2 said Px # -P2 mod kN, then (Px A P2, kN) and (Px - P2, kN) will be\nL413:     nontrivial factors of N.\nL414:     (vii) Some Coding Considerations. Initializing the sieve array when changing\nL415:     polynomials does take some time. Since the sieve is an array of bytes, one should\nL416:     equivalence the sieve array to a full word array of the machine before doing the\nL417:     initialization. One can also combine the array initialization with sieving the smallest\nL418:     primes by selecting an appropriate value with which to initialize the sieve.\nL419:     5. Selection of Input Parameters. The author has found, after many trials, that the\nL420:     following combination of parameters works well (K means 1000):\nL421:     Table 1\nL422:     Number\nL423:     of Digits\nL424:     24\nL425:     30\nL426:     36\nL427:     42\nL428:     48\nL429:     54\nL430:     60\nL431:     66\nL432:     Factor Base\nL433:     Size\nL434:     M\nL435:     T\nL436:     100\nL437:     200\nL438:     400\nL439:     900\nL440:     1200\nL441:     2000\nL442:     3000\nL443:     4500\nL444:     5K\nL445:     25K\nL446:     25K\nL447:     50K\nL448:     100K\nL449:     250K\nL450:     350K\nL451:     500K\nL452:     1.5\nL453:     1.5\nL454:     1.75\nL455:     2.0\nL456:     2.0\nL457:     2.2\nL458:     2.4\nL459:     2.6\nL460:     Median VAX/780\nL461:     Run Time\nL462:     15 sec\nL463:     80 sec\nL464:     400 sec\nL465:     1800 sec\nL466:     8100 sec\nL467:     27600 sec\nL468:     97200 sec\nL469:     360000sec\nL470:     THE MULTIPLE POLYNOMIAL QUADRATIC SIEVE\nL471:     335\nL472:     The multiplier may be evaluated using the following modified version of the\nL473:     Knuth-Schroeppel function:\nL474:     f(k,N) = YaSÍPí,kN) \\og(p) - ilog(Är) for all p, e FB,\nL475:     i\nL476:     g(p,kN) = 2/p\nL477:     iipik,\nL478:     g(2,kN) = 2\nL479:     = l/p\nL480:     if p\\k,\nL481:     =0\nL482:     ifAr = lmod8,\nL483:     otherwise.\nL484:     It was observed in [7] that one might not want to keep all of the large prime\nL485:     factorizations that were found. For this algorithm, the time spent doing the division\nL486:     is significant for small numbers. A large T means the algorithm spends a lot of time\nL487:     constructing factorizations that might not be of much use. This is because the\nL488:     probability of finding a match between very large primes is small.\nL489:     As numbers get larger, the percent of time spent doing the factorizations drops\nL490:     sharply. Since the cost of handling the large primes is small, it is advantageous to\nL491:     keep as many as possible. For reasons of programming convenience and ease of data\nL492:     management we restricted our program to single-precision integers on our machine\nL493:     (32 bits). The value of />maxr is usually larger than 32 bits, however, for numbers\nL494:     greater than 54 digits. The reason for this is to allow a margin for round-off error,\nL495:     since we only work with integer approximations to log(/>). Also, since sieving with\nL496:     respect to prime powers is expensive, we do not do it. Instead, multiple instances of\nL497:     a given prime factor are found when constructing the factorizations. Selecting a\nL498:     larger value of T allows for more multiple prime factors.\nL499:     Also, because of differences in machine architecture, it is desirable to break up the\nL500:     sieve interval into pieces. The optimal size usually depends on the amount of cache\nL501:     memory available. When sieving, one would like to keep global memory references\nL502:     to a minimum and for that purpose should make each piece of the sieve interval\nL503:     small enough to fit in cache.\nL504:     Depending on the quality of the multiplier k and the corresponding value of the\nL505:     modified Knuth-Schroeppel function, one often sees variations in run time of up to a\nL506:     factor of 2.5 for numbers of a given size. Thus, the above times are only approximate\nL507:     and represent typical times that we observed in our computations.\nL508:     6. Some Numerical Results. We present here several factorizations from the\nL509:     MOST/MORE\nL510:     wanted tables of the Cunningham project [1], along with several\nL511:     others of interest (U and V are respectively Fibonacci and Lucas numbers) and the\nL512:     factorization times. These were all obtained with a SUN-3/75. Typically, about 15\nL513:     to 20 percent of the total run time was spent computing coefficients of the\nL514:     polynomials and finding their roots, although the larger numbers took up to 30\nL515:     percent. The processing of the large primes from (15), the matrix reduction step, and\nL516:     the computation of congruence (1) took only a small fraction of the total run time.\nL517:     This post-processing depends only on the size of the factor base and not on the\nL518:     number being factored. For a factor base of 3000 primes, processing the large primes\nL519:     took about 10 minutes, reducing the matrix about 20 minutes, and computing\nL520:     congruence (1) about 1 minute. A factor base of 1000 primes took only about 1\nL521:     minute total for all three steps.\nL522:     336\nL523:     ROBERT D. SILVERMAN\nL524:     The designation\nL525:     a, b + or a, b -\nL526:     means ah A 1 ot ab — 1, respectively, while\nL527:     2,446L is part of the Aurefeullian factorization of 2440+ 1 [1]. The designation Pxx\nL528:     means a prime number of xx digits. Those factorizations marked with * were done\nL529:     using a parallel implementation, described below. The time given for these factorizations is the total time summed over all machines.\nL530:     Table 2\nL531:     Factorization\nL532:     Times\nL533:     TIME\nL534:     (his)\nL535:     0.25\nL536:     2.50\nL537:     2.33\nL538:     3.10\nL539:     4.10\nL540:     3.75\nL541:     6.50\nL542:     11.5\nL543:     10.0\nL544:     14.2\nL545:     15.2\nL546:     22.2*\nL547:     42.1\nL548:     37.5\nL549:     Number\nL550:     Size\nL551:     Factors\nL552:     V298\nL553:     U507\nL554:     U615\nL555:     12,676,915,83 +\nL556:     6,86 +\nL557:     2,224 +\nL558:     3,131 +\nL559:     2,446L\nL560:     2,26311,62 +\nL561:     2,239 +\nL562:     7,796,94 +\nL563:     45\nL564:     53\nL565:     54\nL566:     54\nL567:     56\nL568:     56\nL569:     56\nL570:     58\nL571:     60\nL572:     60\nL573:     62\nL574:     63\nL575:     66\nL576:     66\nL577:     69\nL578:     10,73 +\nL579:     2,272 +\nL580:     6,128 +\nL581:     70\nL582:     74\nL583:     75\nL584:     2,269 +\nL585:     81\nL586:     271293387891105049.P28\nL587:     17340889195212892399797173.P27\nL588:     1846858344247612322281.P33\nL589:     17577834702049211.P38\nL590:     48215910563832798697.P37\nL591:     4029666108840585686296627.P31\nL592:     2914764989376043020733.P35\nL593:     167773885276849215533569.P35\nL594:     114742271